---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const pageTitle = "The Loop — Solutions";
const description =
  "Practical ways young adults can break the loop, alongside ethical responsibilities platforms should take seriously.";
---

<MainLayout title={pageTitle} description={description}>
  <section class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-4xl flex-col gap-10 px-4 py-12">

    <!-- Intro -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        Breaking the loop
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">
        <p>
          Understanding how social media works is an important first step — but awareness
          alone does not automatically change behaviour.
        </p>

        <p>
          Because these platforms are designed around attention, breaking the loop often
          requires small, intentional changes rather than complete disconnection.
        </p>

        <p>
          Solutions exist at two levels: what <strong>individual users</strong> can do,
          and what <strong>platforms themselves</strong> should be responsible for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Individual actions -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        What young adults can do
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Limit notifications</h4>
          <p>
            Notifications are one of the strongest triggers for habitual checking.
            Reducing them — especially non-essential alerts — creates distance between
            impulse and action.
          </p>
          <p>
            Fewer interruptions make it easier to choose when to engage, rather than
            reacting automatically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Switch from “For You” to “Following” feeds</h4>
          <p>
            Algorithmic feeds prioritise engagement, not relevance or wellbeing.
            Switching to chronological or “Following” views reduces exposure to
            emotionally charged or comparison-heavy content.
          </p>
          <p>
            This doesn’t remove algorithms entirely, but it weakens their influence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Use screen time controls</h4>
          <p>
            Built-in screen time tools can make usage visible rather than automatic.
            Setting gentle limits — especially at night — helps disrupt doomscrolling
            and sleep disruption.
          </p>
          <p>
            These tools work best as reminders, not punishments.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Curate content intentionally</h4>
          <p>
            Unfollowing, muting, or marking content as “not interested” sends signals
            back to the system.
          </p>
          <p>
            Over time, this reshapes the feed — reducing exposure to content that fuels
            anxiety, comparison, or negativity.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Platform responsibility -->
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
      <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
        <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
        What platforms should do
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-100 md:text-xl">

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Transparency</h4>
          <p>
            Users deserve clearer explanations of why certain content appears in their
            feed. Transparency helps people understand how their behaviour shapes what
            they see.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Opt-out of personalisation</h4>
          <p>
            Platforms could offer meaningful alternatives to algorithmic feeds —
            not hidden settings, but real, accessible choices.
          </p>
          <p>
            Personalisation should be optional, not unavoidable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Remove dark patterns</h4>
          <p>
            Design patterns that exploit psychological vulnerabilities — infinite
            scroll, constant alerts, streaks — prioritise engagement over wellbeing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Removing or softening these patterns would reduce compulsive use without
            removing functionality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3">
          <h4 class="font-semibold">Age-specific safeguards</h4>
          <p>
            Young users are not simply smaller adults. Platforms could implement
            age-aware defaults, reduced recommendation intensity, and stronger
            protections for vulnerable users.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-700/80 bg-slate-900/70 px-6 py-8 shadow-lg">
        <div class="mb-4 inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-300">
            <span class="h-px w-6 bg-sky-400/70"></span>
            External resources
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
            <p>
            If social media use is affecting your mental health, you’re not alone — and support
            doesn’t have to come only from within the app.
            </p>

            <div class="space-y-4">
            <div>
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-100">Digital wellbeing tools</h4>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6 text-slate-200">
                <li>Built-in screen time tools on iOS and Android</li>
                <li>Focus modes and notification scheduling</li>
                <li>Browser extensions that limit feeds or infinite scroll</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div>
                <h4 class="font-semibold text-slate-100">Mental health support</h4>
                <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6 text-slate-200">
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.samaritans.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-sky-400/50 hover:text-sky-300">
                    Samaritans (UK & ROI)
                    </a>
                    — 24/7 emotional support
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="https://www.mind.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-sky-400/50 hover:text-sky-300">
                    Mind
                    </a>
                    — mental health information and resources
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="https://findahelpline.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline decoration-sky-400/50 hover:text-sky-300">
                    Find a Helpline
                    </a>
                    — international directory of local support services
                </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </article>


    <!-- Digital literacy -->
    <article class="max-w-prose space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-200 md:text-lg">
      <p>
        Beyond individual settings and platform design, <strong>digital literacy</strong>
        plays a crucial role.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding how algorithms work, recognising emotional manipulation, and
        questioning why certain content feels compelling helps users engage more
        critically rather than passively.
      </p>

      <p>
        Digital literacy is not about avoiding social media — it’s about using it with
        awareness, intention, and agency.
      </p>

      <p>
        Breaking the loop does not require disconnecting from the digital world. It
        requires understanding it well enough to step back when necessary.
      </p>
    </article>

  </section>
</MainLayout>
